#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069781} :activity/id #uuid "d5e19120-ea4c-56bb-8467-d5e831ef288e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049587 :topic/id #uuid "0246121f-0f22-5c51-a8c1-62015ed02e05" :topic/math-academy-id 421} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070002 :tutorial/content "A line that crosses two parallel lines is called a **transversal**. For example, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ in the diagram below is a transversal.\n\n![](images/cd/cdae3e21ca7b66c756e047b2f71dd03d1320a947e7477f6b025dacef95321399.png)\n\nA pair of parallel lines with a transversal form a set of $8$ angles, shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4465bbe051f978925e704cc40ced894c4e8c6625c53c7bd944a93c123359ab9.png)\n\nThis set of eight angles can be grouped into two categories:\n\n- We have four pairs of **vertical angles**:![](images/e0/e010db0c586e29e8ae640842f1519da314455bb7520680b80530799509058df3.png)\n\n- We also have four pairs of so-called **corresponding angles**:![](images/d1/d1b3f1ede08e3c20c80b7a23477491237e73a9b6691d93e6deabc5e5a4e0c57b.png)\nCorresponding pairs of angles are located in the same position as each other relative to the parallel lines and on the same side of the transversal. Pairs of corresponding angles are congruent." :tutorial/id #uuid "40b3e634-5345-5152-8f01-f8adc3303078" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6722 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e004f524-3c81-54dc-a543-8d822f7af1a4" :step/math-academy-id 40674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068446 :question/id #uuid "142170e2-0b2d-5612-92d8-7c989aa1453e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f2cc01-7206-5396-aeef-8e4a73809186" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069331}] :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving Two Unknown Corresponding Angles"} :step/id #uuid "15924751-c726-5bb6-a169-80c8a5ad667e" :step/math-academy-id 40675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069788}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068450 :question/id #uuid "40e3c6c8-c86a-55bf-80ef-d8845828ad27" :question/math-academy-id "e-10855"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048174}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069522}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"} :step/id #uuid "3e415bc4-98c6-58c5-9103-42a481f6e39e" :step/math-academy-id 41152 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068452 :question/id #uuid "b983a05e-715b-5541-be19-cefac6f82b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85cc8613-7372-5e19-be2d-afd8d58d4708" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069333}] :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving an Unknown Corresponding Angle"} :step/id #uuid "c594c925-bfd5-53e6-a96b-e13cc0e7650a" :step/math-academy-id 42008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068453 :question/id #uuid "c7d91325-bc8d-571d-8ae7-aa66ae0534c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11055"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cb30b2e-2f71-55a0-a80a-f7a0f5e71780" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069137}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals That Require Solving an Equation"} :step/id #uuid "79d55ed9-0774-5bc8-a780-b7745cc2bd4a" :step/math-academy-id 42009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070008 :tutorial/content "Consider the following pair of parallel lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, and the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$.\n\n![](images/af/af403ef4827e840a49d5a3bde36561d22a508d262b3ad15b830812aefc306286.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $x$?\n\nFirst, note that $\\angle DIF$ and $\\angle BJI$ are corresponding angles. Therefore, they are congruent and thus have the same measure. This means we can write down the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DIF &= m∠BJI \\\\\n3x - 20^{∘} &= x + 10^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first subtract $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 20^{∘} &= x + 10^{∘} \\\\\n3x - 20^{∘} - x &= x + 10^{∘} - x \\\\\n2x - 20^{∘} &= 10^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $20^\\circ$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 20^{∘} &= 10^{∘} \\\\\n2x - 20^{∘} + 20^{∘} &= 10^{∘} + 20^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 30^{∘} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{30^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= 15^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 15^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=15^\\circ$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1983a72b-cd06-525b-8a7f-820171f631c1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7640 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c53425c9-93f8-50f1-ba9b-c5137c0d9d3e" :step/math-academy-id 44334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069790}}] :activity/title "Corresponding Angles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068954}]}
